1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,890
Go back to that refund policy for a second: thirty days, full refund, stated with

2
00:00:04,890 --> 00:00:07,133
total confidence, invented from nothing.

3
00:00:07,133 --> 00:00:11,785
Notice what it never said, even once: did anything in that sentence sound like "I'm

4
00:00:11,785 --> 00:00:12,346
not sure"?

5
00:00:12,346 --> 00:00:16,718
That has a name, and understanding the name properly is the difference between

6
00:00:16,718 --> 00:00:21,427
explaining it well to a skeptical engineer and just saying "AI does that sometimes."

7
00:00:21,427 --> 00:00:24,005
Hallucination is not the model malfunctioning.

8
00:00:24,005 --> 00:00:28,546
It's the model doing the exact same thing it does when it's right: predicting the

9
00:00:28,546 --> 00:00:31,181
most plausible continuation of the text so far.

10
00:00:31,181 --> 00:00:35,441
The only difference is that this time, nothing in its training or its prompt

11
00:00:35,441 --> 00:00:39,981
anchored "plausible" to "true." So it filled the gap with whatever sounded right.

12
00:00:39,981 --> 00:00:44,466
That distinction matters, because it tells you the fix isn't "make the model try

13
00:00:44,466 --> 00:00:48,446
harder." It's giving it something true to anchor to in the first place.

14
00:00:48,446 --> 00:00:51,697
It also matters for a conversation you will actually have.

15
00:00:51,697 --> 00:00:56,293
Someone will call a wrong answer "a glitch" or "a bug they'll patch." Neither word

16
00:00:56,293 --> 00:01:00,946
is accurate, and using the accurate one is how you get taken seriously in the room.

17
00:01:01,496 --> 00:01:05,402
Here's why you can't patch this out with a stricter prompt alone.

18
00:01:05,402 --> 00:01:10,389
There's no separate "I don't know" system sitting off to the side, waiting to catch

19
00:01:10,389 --> 00:01:10,870
the gap.

20
00:01:10,870 --> 00:01:14,174
It's the same single loop from lecture one, every time.

21
00:01:14,174 --> 00:01:18,741
If the context you gave it actually contains the answer, that same mechanism

22
00:01:18,741 --> 00:01:23,428
predicts words anchored to real information, and it's remarkably good at that.

23
00:01:23,428 --> 00:01:28,355
If the context doesn't contain the answer, the exact same mechanism keeps running,

24
00:01:28,355 --> 00:01:30,939
because that's the only mechanism there is.

25
00:01:30,939 --> 00:01:35,806
It predicts the next plausible word anyway, anchored to nothing but "this is what

26
00:01:35,806 --> 00:01:40,493
sentences like this usually say." From the outside, both outputs sound equally

27
00:01:40,493 --> 00:01:42,055
fluent, equally confident.

28
00:01:42,055 --> 00:01:45,420
Only one of them is actually connected to anything true.

29
00:01:45,420 --> 00:01:50,467
This is also why hallucination gets worse exactly when you'd least expect it: on the

30
00:01:50,467 --> 00:01:52,811
questions that sound the most ordinary.

31
00:01:52,811 --> 00:01:56,656
A visibly strange question makes people double-check the answer.

32
00:01:56,656 --> 00:02:01,523
A completely mundane one, like a refund policy, doesn't, and mundane is precisely

33
00:02:01,523 --> 00:02:03,927
where an invented answer blends in best.

34
00:02:04,477 --> 00:02:09,169
Two shapes you'll actually meet, and they need slightly different fixes, so it's

35
00:02:09,169 --> 00:02:10,636
worth telling them apart.

36
00:02:10,636 --> 00:02:15,270
Knowledge-gap hallucination is the refund policy: ask it something it was never

37
00:02:15,270 --> 00:02:19,728
given the means to know, and it answers from general pattern instead of your

38
00:02:19,728 --> 00:02:20,315
specifics.

39
00:02:20,315 --> 00:02:24,714
Unsupported-detail hallucination is sneakier, because most of the answer is

40
00:02:24,714 --> 00:02:25,829
genuinely grounded.

41
00:02:25,829 --> 00:02:30,697
Give it a real document to summarise, and it gets the substance right, then invents

42
00:02:30,697 --> 00:02:35,625
one small specific, a page number, a date, a quote, that sounds exactly as confident

43
00:02:35,625 --> 00:02:37,033
as everything around it.

44
00:02:37,033 --> 00:02:41,960
That one's harder to catch, because ninety percent of the sentence earned your trust

45
00:02:41,960 --> 00:02:42,488
honestly.

46
00:02:42,488 --> 00:02:46,535
Ask which one you're more worried about, and most teams answer wrong.

47
00:02:46,535 --> 00:02:51,287
They spend their review time hunting knowledge-gap hallucinations, because that's

48
00:02:51,287 --> 00:02:56,097
the dramatic one, and let the quieter unsupported detail through unchecked, on the

49
00:02:56,097 --> 00:02:58,033
document they were sure was safe.

50
00:02:58,583 --> 00:03:03,534
Here's the honest list, and notice what's missing from it: a cleverer system prompt.

51
00:03:03,534 --> 00:03:08,308
Grounding means putting the actual answer inside the context before you ask, your

52
00:03:08,308 --> 00:03:12,905
real refund policy, your real document, so the mechanism has something true to

53
00:03:12,905 --> 00:03:15,145
predict from instead of a gap to fill.

54
00:03:15,145 --> 00:03:18,858
That's most of what Section five, RAG, is built to do properly.

55
00:03:18,858 --> 00:03:23,691
Verification means checking the output against a real source after it's generated,

56
00:03:23,691 --> 00:03:28,465
catching the unsupported detail that slipped through even when most of the answer

57
00:03:28,465 --> 00:03:29,231
was grounded.

58
00:03:29,231 --> 00:03:34,182
That's what the rubric and golden set you'll build in Section four are actually for.

59
00:03:34,732 --> 00:03:39,149
Say the honest caveat, because "this is unfixable" would be its own kind of

60
00:03:39,149 --> 00:03:39,915
overclaiming.

61
00:03:39,915 --> 00:03:42,094
Training has genuinely improved here.

62
00:03:42,094 --> 00:03:46,983
Models today are measurably more willing to say they don't know something than they

63
00:03:46,983 --> 00:03:47,277
were.

64
00:03:47,277 --> 00:03:52,166
That's real progress, and it's worth acknowledging rather than pretending nothing's

65
00:03:52,166 --> 00:03:52,637
changed.

66
00:03:52,637 --> 00:03:57,585
But reduced is not eliminated, and it never will be from training alone, because the

67
00:03:57,585 --> 00:03:59,999
mechanism is still next-token prediction.

68
00:03:59,999 --> 00:04:04,770
Asking it nicely to only say true things changes the words in the prompt, not the

69
00:04:04,770 --> 00:04:06,655
mechanism generating the answer.

70
00:04:06,655 --> 00:04:10,778
Grounding and verification change what the mechanism has to work with.

71
00:04:10,778 --> 00:04:12,486
That's the actual difference.

72
00:04:13,036 --> 00:04:14,745
One thing before you move on.

73
00:04:14,745 --> 00:04:19,637
Find one place in your own feature where it's answering a question without actually

74
00:04:19,637 --> 00:04:21,995
being given the facts to answer it from.

75
00:04:21,995 --> 00:04:26,297
That gap is where your next hallucination is waiting, not hypothetically,

76
00:04:26,297 --> 00:04:27,064
specifically.

77
00:04:27,064 --> 00:04:31,779
Then name which fix closes it: grounding, if the fact exists somewhere you could

78
00:04:31,779 --> 00:04:36,376
retrieve it, or verification, if you can at least check the answer afterwards.

79
00:04:36,376 --> 00:04:40,738
If neither is possible yet, that's worth knowing before launch, not after.

80
00:04:40,738 --> 00:04:45,571
Next lecture turns this into a map: seven archetypes of AI feature, and which ones

81
00:04:45,571 --> 00:04:47,398
actually make it to production.
